BANK.push(
{"id":"m1","d":2,"q":"A defense contractor is building a labeled document system for an intelligence program. Analysts cleared to SECRET routinely read CONFIDENTIAL source reports and must also append their findings to a TOP SECRET fusion report maintained by a senior cell. The accreditor's main concern, raised after a near miss last quarter, is that SECRET text pasted into a CONFIDENTIAL working draft would leak downward. The program office prefers a single formal rule the trusted kernel can enforce. Which rule fits these needs BEST?","o":["The Bell-LaPadula *-property, which stops a subject from writing to objects below its current level","The Bell-LaPadula strong star property, which limits a subject to reading and writing at its own level","The Bell-LaPadula simple security property, which stops a subject from reading objects above its level","The Biba *-integrity axiom, which stops a subject from writing to objects above its integrity level"],"a":[0],"e":"The *-property blocks write down, which is the leak the accreditor cares about, and still allows the needed write up to the TOP SECRET fusion report. The strong star property would also block write down but would break the required append to the higher report. Simple security controls reading up, not the downward write. Biba is an integrity model and blocks write up, the opposite of what is needed."},
{"id":"m2","d":2,"q":"A pharmaceutical lab system enters assay results only through a validated application that checks instrument data, and the approver must differ from the analyst. During a GxP audit, the inspector finds that the on-call DBA can run UPDATE statements directly against the results table to fix stuck records, and has done so twice this year. The nightly reconciliation job reported no issues. In Clark-Wilson terms, which requirement does the DBA path MOST directly violate?","o":["The certification rule requiring integrity verification procedures to confirm the CDIs are in a valid state","The enforcement rule that users may change CDIs only through certified transformation procedures","The certification rule requiring that transformation procedures write to an append-only log CDI","The enforcement rule requiring the system to authenticate each user before a procedure is run"],"a":[1],"e":"Clark-Wilson's core control is the access triple: a user may touch a constrained data item only through an authorized transformation procedure. Direct SQL bypasses the TP, so that enforcement rule is the one broken. IVPs check validity after the fact, and the clean nightly run shows why they are not enough. Logging and authentication matter, but the DBA was authenticated and logging does not stop the bypass."},
{"id":"m3","d":2,"q":"A consulting firm advises two competing regional banks and several unrelated retailers. Any consultant may be staffed on either bank, and staffing changes weekly, so the firm cannot predict assignments in advance. Once a consultant opens Bank A's working papers, the firm's ethics policy says that person must not open Bank B's papers for the life of the engagement, though retail clients stay open to them. The document platform already supports roles and attributes. Which model BEST captures this rule?","o":["Role-based access control with static separation of duty between the two bank roles","Mandatory access control with a separate compartment assigned to each bank client","Brewer-Nash, with both banks placed in one conflict-of-interest class","Attribute-based control keyed to the consultant's current staffing record"],"a":[2],"e":"The restriction depends on what the consultant has already accessed, not on a fixed assignment, which is the Chinese Wall idea. Brewer-Nash tracks access history and blocks other datasets in the same conflict class. Static SoD needs assignments known in advance. Compartments grant access by clearance, not history. An attribute tied to the current staffing record changes weekly and would let a consultant move from Bank A to Bank B."},
{"id":"m4","d":2,"q":"A water utility is designing a high-assurance controller. Field sensors run at a low integrity level and the controller's setpoint configuration is high integrity. Sensors must not alter the configuration, but the controller has to read raw sensor telemetry to do its job, and it must keep writing its own high integrity setpoints after doing so. The vendor's kernel supports lattice-based integrity labels, and the team does not want a transaction layer. Which Biba policy fits BEST?","o":["Strict integrity policy, combining the simple integrity axiom with the *-integrity axiom","Low-water-mark policy, lowering the controller's level after it reads low integrity data","Ring policy, which permits reads at all levels but blocks writes upward","Clark-Wilson certified transformation procedures wrapped around each sensor update"],"a":[2],"e":"The ring policy lets the controller read low integrity telemetry while still blocking sensors from writing up into the configuration. Strict integrity blocks read down, so the controller could not read telemetry. Low-water-mark would demote the controller after each read, and it could then no longer write its high integrity setpoints. Clark-Wilson could work but the team explicitly ruled out a transaction layer."},
{"id":"m5","d":2,"q":"A retailer completed a TOGAF ADM cycle last year and the resulting payment architecture is in production under Phase G governance. A new card brand mandate now requires stronger customer authentication for certain transactions by next spring. The security architect must decide whether this can be handled as a change to the current architecture or needs a new ADM cycle with a revised vision. The CIO also wants the store Wi-Fi refresh discussed at the same board. In which ADM phase is that decision formally made?","o":["Requirements Management, which stores and routes requirements across all phases","Phase A, Architecture Vision, which sets scope and secures stakeholder approval","Phase G, Implementation Governance, which checks projects for conformance","Phase H, Architecture Change Management, which assesses change and its impact"],"a":[3],"e":"Phase H receives change drivers after deployment and decides whether to handle them as simplification or incremental change, or to start a new cycle. Requirements Management tracks and feeds requirements but does not make that call. Phase A would only run if Phase H decides a new cycle is needed. Phase G oversees conformance of current implementation work, not whether a new driver warrants re-architecting. The Wi-Fi item is noise."},
{"id":"m6","d":2,"q":"During threat modeling of a claims microservice, the team finds the service reads a role claim from a JWT issued by the corporate IdP but skips signature checks because the API gateway in front of it was assumed to validate tokens. The gateway actually only does rate limiting. A user could change the claim from 'viewer' to 'adjuster' and approve claims. Release is in two weeks and the service mesh project is still a quarter away. What should the architect require FIRST?","o":["Enforce mutual TLS between the gateway and the service through the planned service mesh rollout","Validate the token signature, issuer, audience, and algorithm in the service before using any claim","Shorten token lifetime to five minutes and rotate refresh tokens on each use to limit replay windows","Alert the SOC when an adjuster-level claim appears from a client with no history of using that role"],"a":[1],"e":"The flaw is tampering with an unverified claim that leads to elevation of privilege. The direct fix is for the service to verify the token before trusting it. mTLS authenticates the gateway, not the claims, and it is a quarter away. Short lifetimes do not stop a user from editing a fresh token. Alerting is detective and would fire after an unauthorized approval."},
{"id":"m7","d":2,"q":"An insurer wants to threat model a new customer portal. The CISO insists the process start from business objectives and compliance drivers, include stages for vulnerability analysis and simulating realistic attacks against the application, and end with risks ranked by business impact so the board can fund fixes. The development team already uses STRIDE checklists in sprint planning, and the risk office runs OCTAVE workshops yearly. Which methodology BEST meets the CISO's requirements for this portal?","o":["PASTA, a seven-stage risk-centric process that runs from business objectives through attack modeling","OCTAVE Allegro, a streamlined asset-focused information risk assessment run in workshops","Trike, which builds a requirements model of acceptable actions and derives risks from that model","STRIDE per element, applied to a data flow diagram of the portal and its integrations"],"a":[0],"e":"PASTA starts from business objectives, includes vulnerability analysis and attack simulation stages, and ends with business-impact risk analysis, which matches every requirement. OCTAVE Allegro is organizational and asset-focused and does not simulate attacks on an application. Trike is risk-based but centers on a stakeholder-defined acceptable-use model rather than attack simulation tied to business objectives. STRIDE finds threat categories but does not rank by business impact on its own."},
{"id":"m8","d":2,"q":"A health startup has a STRIDE threat model and data flow diagram for its new mobile symptom tracker. Before launch in the EU, the DPO says the team has not analyzed whether users could be linked across datasets, re-identified from location traces, or detected as users of a sensitive service just by their network traffic. A DPIA template is already drafted for the regulator. Which step BEST fills the analysis gap the DPO described?","o":["Complete the GDPR Article 35 DPIA and submit it to the regulator with planned mitigations","Apply LINDDUN to the existing data flow diagram to find privacy threats per element","Extend the STRIDE information disclosure analysis to cover location data flows","Build a NIST Privacy Framework target profile and compare it with current practice"],"a":[1],"e":"LINDDUN systematically covers linkability, identifiability, detectability, and the other privacy threat categories, and it reuses the DFD the team already has. The DPIA is required but is a risk assessment and record, not a method for finding these threats. STRIDE disclosure focuses on confidentiality and misses linkability and detectability. A Privacy Framework profile describes outcomes, not threat enumeration."},
{"id":"m9","d":2,"q":"A document service caches authorization decisions for 30 minutes to keep response times under 50 ms. Legal has added a requirement that revoked access to litigation holds take effect within 60 seconds. Load testing shows the policy engine can handle roughly ten times current traffic. The team proposes several changes before the audit next month. Which change BEST restores the design principle that the 30-minute cache undermines?","o":["Tighten role definitions so that fewer users hold litigation hold access in the first place","Re-authenticate users each 30 minutes so their sessions reflect current status","Invalidate cached decisions on revocation events and cap cache lifetime below 60 seconds","Move all authorization into the API gateway so decisions are made in one place"],"a":[2],"e":"Long-lived cached decisions weaken complete mediation because access is not checked against current policy. Event-driven invalidation with a short TTL brings checks back in line with the 60-second rule, and the engine has headroom. Narrower roles support least privilege but do not fix stale decisions. Re-authentication proves identity, not current authorization. Centralizing in the gateway still leaves the same stale cache."},
{"id":"m10","d":2,"q":"A design review finds that the internet-facing customer portal and the internal admin console use the same in-house authentication library and the same session signing key. The admin console is reachable only from the management network. A recent bug bounty report showed a session forging flaw in the library, now patched. The team asks for a structural change to limit the impact of the next library flaw. Which change BEST applies the relevant design principle?","o":["Put the admin console behind a VPN with device posture checks required for all administrators","Add SAST and dependency scanning gates to the library's build pipeline before release","Give the admin console its own authentication path and signing keys in a separate trust domain","Place a WAF in front of the portal with virtual patching rules for authentication attacks"],"a":[2],"e":"Least common mechanism says shared mechanisms create shared fate. A separate path and keys mean a forged portal session is not valid on the console. The console is already network restricted, so a VPN adds little against forged sessions arriving through allowed paths. Build scanning and a WAF reduce the chance of flaws but leave both systems tied to one mechanism and one key."},
{"id":"m11","d":2,"q":"An architect is reviewing a hypervisor-based enforcement layer that is meant to act as a reference monitor for workloads holding regulated data. The vendor's design document lists several characteristics, and the review board wants to know which ones conflict with reference monitor requirements. The vendor plans to submit the product for formal evaluation next year. Which characteristic is of LEAST concern against those requirements?","o":["Policy updates arrive as signed bundles that only authorized administrators can approve","A fast path skips policy checks for VMs that are tagged as trusted at deployment time","The enforcement module shares writable memory pages with the paravirtualized guest drivers","The policy engine is about two million lines of code with no plan for formal analysis"],"a":[0],"e":"Signed, administrator-approved policy updates are normal and support tamper protection. A fast path that skips checks breaks the requirement that mediation is always invoked. Writable memory shared with guest drivers threatens the tamperproof requirement. Two million unanalyzed lines conflict with the requirement that the mechanism be small enough to verify."},
{"id":"m12","d":2,"q":"An enterprise architecture team classifies its artifacts with the Zachman Framework. The security architect is writing down the rule, stated by the claims business owner, that any payout above 50,000 dollars needs approval by a second manager from a different region. The rule must be captured in business terms before any system design exists. The org chart showing claims managers by region was filed last month. In which Zachman cell does this artifact BEST belong?","o":["Business Management row, Who (people) column","Architect row, Why column","Business Management row, Why column","Engineer row, How column"],"a":[2],"e":"The rule comes from the business owner's perspective and states motivation and business rules, which is the Why column in the business row. The Who column holds the roles and organization, like the org chart already filed. The Architect row would be the logical system expression of the rule, which does not exist yet. The Engineer row How column holds physical process or program design."},
{"id":"m13","d":2,"q":"A bank's external auditors ask the project to show, for each of 140 security requirements, which design element implements it and which test proves it. They also want to see whether any security components exist that no requirement asked for. The team has a STRIDE threat model, a test plan, design documents, and a recent penetration test report with eight medium findings. Which artifact should the architect produce to answer the auditors?","o":["A bidirectional requirements traceability matrix linking requirements, design elements, and tests","A control mapping of the design against NIST SP 800-53 with implementation status for each","An updated threat model mapping each identified threat to the component and control that mitigates it","A retest of the eight penetration findings with evidence attached for each closed item"],"a":[0],"e":"Only a bidirectional traceability matrix shows forward coverage from requirement to design and test, and backward links that expose components with no requirement. A control mapping to 800-53 traces to an outside catalog, not to the project's 140 requirements. The threat model maps threats, not requirements. Retesting findings is good hygiene but does not answer the coverage question."},
{"id":"m14","d":2,"q":"A hospital group is building its security architecture with SABSA. Workshops with clinical and finance leaders produced attributes such as 'available', 'auditable', and 'patient-safe', each with a measurement approach and a target. The architect must place this business attributes profile correctly in the SABSA matrix so that later control objectives and the information model trace to it. The workshops ran over budget by two weeks. Where does the profile sit in the SABSA matrix?","o":["Contextual layer, Assets (What) column","Conceptual layer, Assets (What) column","Conceptual layer, Motivation (Why) column","Logical layer, Assets (What) column"],"a":[1],"e":"In the SABSA matrix the business attributes profile is the Conceptual layer entry in the Assets column. The Contextual layer Assets cell is the business itself and its decisions. Conceptual Motivation holds control objectives, which are derived next. The Logical layer Assets cell holds the business information model. The budget overrun is noise."},
{"id":"m15","d":2,"q":"An attack tree for 'exfiltrate customer database' has three OR branches. Branch one is AND(phish an admin, cost 1k; bypass push MFA, cost 15k). Branch two is SQL injection in a legacy reporting endpoint, cost 3k. Branch three is bribing a DBA, cost 40k. The team can fund one control this quarter and its stated goal is to raise the cheapest attack cost as far as possible. Which control BEST meets that goal?","o":["Replace push MFA with phishing-resistant FIDO2 keys for all administrators","Fix the reporting endpoint with parameterized queries and retire it","Add DBA session recording with dual approval for bulk export jobs","Deploy egress filtering on the database subnet to block unknown hosts"],"a":[1],"e":"The cheapest path is the 3k SQL injection. Closing it raises the minimum attacker cost to 16k, the phishing branch. Stronger MFA hardens a branch that is already more expensive, so the minimum stays at 3k. DBA controls address the 40k branch. Egress filtering on the database subnet does not stop data leaving through the web application's own responses."},
{"id":"m16","d":2,"q":"A design review for an internal HR platform lists four decisions. New API endpoints stay unreachable until someone adds an explicit allow rule. When the policy decision point times out, enforcement points return HTTP 403. Each request is checked against policy even when the user called the same endpoint seconds earlier. Payroll changes need approval from two separate managers. The reviewer wants to cite the principle behind each one. Which decision is the clearest example of fail-safe defaults?","o":["Returning HTTP 403 whenever the policy decision point times out","Keeping new endpoints unreachable until an explicit allow rule exists","Checking policy on each request even for very recent repeat calls","Requiring two separate managers to sign off on each payroll change request"],"a":[1],"e":"Fail-safe defaults means access is based on explicit permission, so new endpoints start denied. Returning 403 on timeout is fail secure behavior during a failure, a related but different idea. Checking each request is complete mediation. Two approvers is separation of privilege."},
{"id":"m17","d":2,"q":"A medical device maker is designing an infusion pump that authenticates clinicians against a hospital service over Wi-Fi. Field data shows some wards lose wireless for up to an hour during storms. Clinicians must be able to stop or adjust an infusion within drug library limits during an emergency, and FDA premarket guidance expects security controls not to compromise safety. Marketing wants a new color touchscreen in the same release. Which design approach is BEST?","o":["Run redundant authentication servers in two data centers to raise the authentication service's overall uptime","Allow local stop and bounded adjustment in an audited emergency mode, and fail closed otherwise","Cache recent clinician credentials on the pump so that offline logins work during wireless outages","Provide a physical override key held by the charge nurse on each ward to unlock the pump locally"],"a":[1],"e":"A restricted, audited local mode keeps the safety-critical actions available when the network is down while still failing closed for everything else. Redundant servers do not help when the pump itself has no Wi-Fi. Cached credentials only help clinicians who logged in recently and put credential material on a shared device. A charge nurse key adds delay in an emergency. The touchscreen is noise."},
{"id":"m18","d":2,"q":"A federal agency wants to buy a firewall that holds a Common Criteria certificate against a NIAP Protection Profile. Procurement has already confirmed the product appears on NIAP's Product Compliant List. The agency plans to manage the firewalls from the vendor's cloud console and to enable a new SD-WAN feature released after the evaluation. The vendor also offers a FIPS 140-3 certificate for its crypto module. What should the security architect check FIRST?","o":["Whether the crypto module's FIPS 140-3 certificate covers the exact firmware build being purchased","Whether the cloud console and the SD-WAN feature fall inside the evaluated TOE and its configuration","Whether an assurance continuity maintenance report exists for the current product software version","Whether an internal penetration test of the firewall in the agency's lab confirms the vendor's claims"],"a":[1],"e":"A CC certificate only covers the target of evaluation in its evaluated configuration. If the cloud console and the new feature are outside it, the certificate says little about how the agency will run the product, so scope comes first. FIPS coverage and maintenance reports matter but are follow-on checks. A lab test is useful later and does not replace knowing what was evaluated."},
{"id":"m19","d":2,"q":"A logistics company must expose a legacy shipment service to partners within six weeks. The service runs on a vendor appliance that cannot be modified or have agents installed, speaks an older XML protocol, and accepts a static shared password. Partners need certificate-based authentication and schema validation of each request before it reaches the appliance. A full replacement is planned for next year. Which pattern BEST fits this situation?","o":["A sidecar proxy deployed alongside the appliance to add mTLS and validation","A strangler fig migration that replaces the appliance's functions in stages","A hardened gateway in front of the appliance enforcing auth and validation","A bulkhead that isolates partner traffic into its own pool of resources"],"a":[2],"e":"A gateway acting as a policy enforcement point can add certificate authentication and schema checks without touching the appliance. A sidecar needs to run with the workload, which the appliance does not allow. A strangler fig is the right path for next year's replacement but will not meet six weeks. A bulkhead limits failure spread but does not authenticate or validate."},
{"id":"m20","d":2,"q":"A data science team asks to deviate from the company's cloud reference architecture because they need a managed GPU service that the reference does not cover. Their design has a sound threat model, and the team lead has a strong record. The review board meets weekly, and the reference architecture is due for its yearly revision in five months. The team's sprint demo is next Friday. What should the security architect do?","o":["Approve the deviation at the next board meeting based on the strength of the team's threat model","Update the reference architecture now to add a GPU pattern before the team is allowed to proceed","Ask the team to redesign around the approved compute patterns that the reference already includes","Use the exception process with a named risk owner, and feed the pattern into the next revision"],"a":[3],"e":"The exception process documents the residual risk, assigns an owner, and lets the team move, while the yearly revision captures the new pattern for others. Approving on reputation skips risk ownership. Rewriting the reference now delays the team and rushes a standard. Forcing existing patterns may not meet the business need for GPU workloads."},
{"id":"m21","d":2,"q":"A university's steering committee approved the target security architecture last month, and the current-state baseline was finished last week. The fiscal year budget submission is due in six weeks. The CIO also mentions that a zero trust initiative has been gaining support among deans and might reshape priorities. The architect has a long list of differences between current and target. What should the architect do NEXT?","o":["Rank the gaps by risk and dependency, then group them into a sequenced roadmap of work","Issue RFPs for the three biggest gaps so that firm pricing is ready for the budget","Commission a penetration test to confirm the current-state baseline is accurate","Revise the target state so that it reflects the emerging zero trust initiative first"],"a":[0],"e":"With an approved target and a finished baseline, the next step is to rank the gaps and turn them into work packages and a roadmap that can drive the budget. RFPs come after you know which gaps matter most. The baseline is already done, and a pen test would delay. Reopening an approved target for an idea that has not been decided is scope creep."},
{"id":"m22","d":2,"q":"A hospital's new badge-tap login met every test case in its specification, including the 40-second maximum authentication time the spec allowed. In the pilot, nurses started sharing a logged-in session per cart because 40 seconds per tap was too slow during medication rounds. The vendor points out that the build matches the signed-off spec, and the pilot ward also had a short printer outage. How should the architect classify this, and what follows?","o":["A verification failure, so fix the implementation until it meets the specification","A validation failure, so revisit the requirements with clinical stakeholders","A policy issue, so retrain nurses and enforce the no-sharing rule on carts","A performance issue, so tune the badge service to beat the 40-second limit"],"a":[1],"e":"The system was built to spec, so verification passed. It still does not meet the real need, which is a validation failure, and the fix starts with the requirements and the people who use the system. Retraining treats the symptom and ignores why users work around the control. Tuning without changing the requirement may help but skips the root cause in the spec."},
{"id":"m23","d":2,"q":"A US defense program office is building its DoDAF 2.02 architecture description for a new mission system. The security architect must record which security standards apply today, such as FIPS 140-3 and CNSSI 1253, and forecast which ones are expected to change over the system's lifecycle so that designs stay compliant. The program also tracks contract milestones in a separate tool. In which DoDAF viewpoint does this information BEST belong?","o":["The All Viewpoint, which gives the overview and summary of the architecture","The Systems Viewpoint, which describes systems, their links, and functions","The Standards Viewpoint, which holds current and forecast standards profiles","The Project Viewpoint, which ties programs and milestones to capabilities"],"a":[2],"e":"The Standards Viewpoint (StdV-1 and StdV-2) records the standards that apply now and the ones forecast to change. The All Viewpoint gives overview and a dictionary. The Systems Viewpoint describes systems and interfaces, not the standards profile. The Project Viewpoint covers programs and timelines, which is where the milestone tracking fits."},
{"id":"m24","d":2,"q":"A team has a two-hour threat modeling slot for an order platform. The data flow diagram shows a browser calling a web tier, an order service, an order database, a nightly batch job inside the same VPC, and a new inbound callback from a payment partner over the internet. The browser to web tier path follows the standard WAF pattern and was modeled last quarter. Which part of the diagram should get detailed STRIDE analysis FIRST?","o":["The order database, since it holds the most sensitive customer records","The nightly batch job, since it runs unattended with broad read access","The partner's inbound callback, since it newly crosses a trust boundary","The browser to web tier flow, since it faces the largest user population"],"a":[2],"e":"Threats concentrate where data crosses trust boundaries, and the partner callback is a new, unmodeled crossing from the internet. The database matters, but it sits inside the boundary and its exposure flows through the crossings. The batch job stays within the same VPC trust zone. The browser path was modeled recently and follows a standard pattern."},
{"id":"m25","d":2,"q":"A research team is analyzing a capability-based operating system. They represent subjects and objects as nodes in a directed graph, with edges labeled with rights, and the system lets a subject acquire rights from another node it holds rights over, or pass its own rights along. They need a model in which deciding whether a given subject can eventually obtain a given right is tractable, ideally in linear time. Which model fits BEST?","o":["Harrison-Ruzzo-Ullman, which models rights changes with commands on a matrix","Take-Grant, which models rights transfer as rewriting rules over a directed graph","Graham-Denning, which defines primitive protection rules for managing rights","Brewer-Nash, which grants access based on a subject's prior access history"],"a":[1],"e":"Take-Grant models rights as labeled graph edges with take and grant rules, and its safety question can be decided in linear time. HRU is more general and its safety question is undecidable in general. Graham-Denning defines how rights are created, deleted, and transferred but does not give this graph-based decision result. Brewer-Nash addresses conflicts of interest."},
{"id":"m26","d":2,"q":"A retailer's architects have drafted a target design for an omnichannel platform, and a threat model lists 60 threats. Nothing has been built yet, but a staging copy of the current website exists. The security architect wants independent assurance that the design mitigates the modeled threats before the board approves funding in three weeks. A separate team is formally verifying the new token exchange protocol. Which activity is MOST appropriate now?","o":["A penetration test of the staging copy of the current website by an outside firm","A design review tracing each modeled threat to its planned controls and gaps","A tabletop exercise walking leaders through a breach of the new platform","A model-checking analysis of the design's main authentication protocol"],"a":[1],"e":"Before anything is built, a structured review that traces threats to planned controls is the way to show whether the design covers them. A pen test of the current site tests a different system. A tabletop tests response, not design coverage. Model checking is strong assurance for one protocol, which is already being done, but it does not cover the other threats."},
{"id":"m27","d":2,"q":"A bank is splitting a monolithic lending application into about 40 microservices running in one Kubernetes cluster on a flat pod network. A reviewer worries that one compromised service could reach all the others and the shared data stores. The product owner wants to keep independent deployments, and an API gateway with a WAF already protects north-south traffic. Which design measure BEST addresses the reviewer's concern?","o":["Add a WAF rule set tuned for the lending APIs at the existing edge gateway","Segment the cluster nodes into three VLANs by tier with firewall rules between them","Use workload identity with mTLS and per-service authorization on east-west calls","Merge the services into a few larger services to cut the number of interfaces"],"a":[2],"e":"The concern is lateral movement inside the cluster. Strong workload identity, mutual TLS, and per-service authorization policies limit what a compromised service can call. The edge WAF only sees north-south traffic. Node VLANs are too coarse for pods on a shared network. Merging services reduces interfaces but gives up the independence the product owner wants."},
{"id":"m28","d":2,"q":"A trading firm protects the master key in its HSM with a 3-of-5 quorum: five custodians each hold a smart card, and any three must be present to activate the key. Each custodian also signs a yearly attestation, and the firm's auditor wants the design principle named precisely in the security architecture document. The HSM was recently moved to a new data hall. Which principle does the quorum design MOST directly apply?","o":["Separation of duties, since conflicting business tasks are split among the staff","Separation of privilege, since the action needs multiple independent conditions","Least privilege, since each custodian holds only the minimum rights for the role","Defense in depth, since several layers of protection surround the master key"],"a":[1],"e":"Saltzer and Schroeder's separation of privilege means an action needs more than one condition, such as several key holders, before it proceeds. Separation of duties splits conflicting business functions across roles, which is related but organizational. Least privilege is about limiting each person's rights. Defense in depth layers different controls rather than requiring joint action."},
{"id":"m29","d":2,"q":"A startup's vendor offers a proprietary cipher for a new product line, claiming it is stronger because its design is secret, and offers a steep discount. The product will be sold to US federal agencies next year, and the sales team has already promised encryption that meets federal requirements. The engineering lead notes that TLS 1.3 is already used for transport. What should the security architect recommend?","o":["Commission independent cryptanalysis of the proprietary cipher under NDA before adopting it","Use the proprietary cipher inside the existing TLS 1.3 tunnel as an added protective layer","Require a published, approved algorithm like AES-GCM inside a FIPS 140-3 validated module","Accept the cipher but require the vendor to place its specification in escrow"],"a":[2],"e":"Kerckhoffs's principle says security should rest on the key, not on keeping the design secret, and federal buyers need approved algorithms in validated modules. Private cryptanalysis is costly and still would not satisfy FIPS. Layering inside TLS adds complexity and does not make the product compliant. Escrow handles vendor failure, not strength or compliance."},
{"id":"m30","d":2,"q":"Employees at a manufacturer write passwords on sticky notes because policy requires 20 random characters changed every 30 days. The help desk logs about 400 lockouts a week, and an audit flagged notes on monitors in the plant office. The CISO wants a change aligned with current NIST SP 800-63B guidance. Plant floor kiosks are also due for replacement next year. Which change BEST addresses the root problem?","o":["Keep the policy and roll out an enterprise password manager to all staff","Run desk audits for written passwords and apply discipline to repeat cases","Move rotation to 90 days and reduce the minimum length to 14 characters","Drop forced rotation, screen passphrases against breach lists, and add MFA"],"a":[3],"e":"The policy ignores psychological acceptability, so users work around it. SP 800-63B advises against forced periodic changes and composition rules, favors length with blocklist screening, and pairs passwords with MFA. A password manager helps but leaves the burdensome policy in place. Discipline treats the symptom. Softer rotation is still periodic rotation without breach screening."},
{"id":"m31","d":2,"q":"A biotech firm will run genomic analytics in confidential VMs at a cloud provider. A key management service releases dataset decryption keys to each analytics job at startup. The provider has a strong SOC 2 report, the jobs run in a dedicated VPC, and every host gets a client certificate from the firm's PKI. The architect worries that a modified analytics image could request keys and leak data. What should the key service require before releasing a key?","o":["A valid mTLS client certificate proving the request comes from a firm-managed host","A source address inside the dedicated VPC range assigned exclusively to the analytics workloads","A hardware-signed attestation report whose code measurement and TCB version match policy","A current SOC 2 Type II report confirming the provider's controls over its host fleet"],"a":[2],"e":"Remote attestation gives cryptographic evidence that the expected, unmodified code is running in a genuine TEE at an acceptable patch level, which is exactly the modified-image concern. A host certificate proves which host asked, not what code is running. A VPC address is a network location. A SOC 2 report covers the provider's controls, not the specific workload's integrity."},
{"id":"m32","d":2,"q":"A review board is choosing between two designs for a customer identity platform that must pass a formal third-party evaluation next year. Both use OIDC and passkeys, both give each tenant a dedicated session store, and both have the same login experience for users. Design A adds a workflow engine, a plugin system, and six more integrations. Design B has fewer components and a much smaller codebase. Which principle MOST favors Design B here?","o":["Least common mechanism","Open design","Economy of mechanism","Psychological acceptability"],"a":[2],"e":"Economy of mechanism favors small, simple designs that can be reviewed and tested thoroughly, which matters for a formal evaluation. Least common mechanism does not separate the designs because both isolate session stores per tenant. Open design is met equally since both use published standards. Psychological acceptability is the same because the user experience matches."},
{"id":"m33","d":2,"q":"An assessment shows that a perimeter firewall is the only control in front of a customer records database. Internal staff, VPN users, and three partner networks can all reach the database port directly, though only the two application servers need to. The database already uses transparent data encryption, and the CFO has approved a modest budget for one improvement this quarter. Which improvement BEST adds a needed layer?","o":["Upgrade the perimeter firewall to a next-generation model with IPS signatures","Place the database in its own segment that only the application servers can reach","Require MFA for VPN users before they can connect to the internal network","Rotate the database encryption keys and move them into a managed cloud HSM service"],"a":[1],"e":"The core gap is that many networks reach the database directly. Segmenting it so only the application tier can connect adds a real inner layer and cuts exposure for internal, VPN, and partner paths at once. A better perimeter firewall is still a single layer. VPN MFA leaves internal and partner paths open. Encryption at rest does not stop access through the live database service."},
{"id":"m34","d":2,"q":"A government program must consolidate SECRET and UNCLASSIFIED workloads onto shared hardware to meet budget. Testing shows a low-level process can infer high-level activity by timing CPU cache hits. The low-level side runs native code that can build its own high-resolution timers from a counting thread. The program cannot fund separate hosts for each level this year. Which mitigation BEST addresses this channel?","o":["Reduce the timer resolution exposed by the OS to low-level processes on the shared hosts","Turn on full memory encryption so that the high-level workload's memory pages are protected","Partition cache and cores by security level and stop sharing hyperthreads across levels","Move the high-level workload into a separate VM with its own virtual network interfaces"],"a":[2],"e":"This is a covert timing channel through a shared cache. Partitioning the shared resource and not co-scheduling levels on sibling threads removes the signal. Coarser timers help only when the attacker cannot build a timer, and here they can. Memory encryption protects contents, not timing. A separate VM on the same cores still shares the cache."},
{"id":"m35","d":2,"q":"An IoT company has 60 agile teams building device firmware, mobile apps, and a cloud backend. The CISO wants threat models for each application plus a separate view of operational infrastructure, produced in a repeatable way that fits two-week sprints and feeds the DevOps toolchain. The central security team has four people. A board member keeps asking about OCTAVE because his previous company used it. Which methodology fits BEST?","o":["PASTA, run as a full seven-stage analysis for each team's application","OCTAVE Allegro, run as facilitated risk workshops with each business unit","VAST, with automated application and operational threat models at scale","STRIDE per element, run by the central team on each product's diagrams"],"a":[2],"e":"VAST was designed to scale threat modeling across many agile teams with tool support, and it separates application and operational models. PASTA is thorough but heavy to run per application in each sprint. OCTAVE Allegro is an organizational risk assessment, not an application threat model. A four-person central team cannot run STRIDE for 60 teams. The board member's preference is noise."},
{"id":"m36","d":2,"q":"A bank must decide within four months whether to rehost or retire a COBOL claims application on its mainframe. There is no design documentation, the original developers retired, and RACF profiles have grown for 20 years. The architect needs an accurate current-state security model to inform the decision. The vendor also offers a generic mainframe reference architecture. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Adopt the vendor's mainframe reference architecture as a working current-state model","Start the target-state design so that the rehost option can be costed in parallel","Interview operators and pull RACF and SMF data to map actual flows and access paths","Run an authenticated vulnerability scan and build the model from the findings list"],"a":[2],"e":"With no documentation, the current state has to be reverse-engineered from people, configuration, and logs, then checked against observed activity. A reference architecture shows how things should be, not how this system is. Target design without a baseline risks missing hidden dependencies. A scan lists weaknesses but does not show flows, trust relationships, or access paths."},
{"id":"m37","d":2,"q":"A team is building a cross-domain guard and drawing the boundary of its security kernel. Reviewers want the kernel as small as possible so it can be formally analyzed, while still implementing the reference monitor. Candidate components include label checks on each transfer, the code that binds labels to objects, the domain switching code, and an operator console that displays audit records. Which component should sit OUTSIDE the kernel?","o":["The label checks applied to each message transfer between domains","The code that binds security labels to objects and protects them","The domain switching code that moves execution between privilege levels","The operator console that formats and displays recorded audit events"],"a":[3],"e":"The security kernel holds only the hardware, firmware, and software that implement the reference monitor. Label checks, label binding, and domain switching are needed for mediation and must be inside. A console that shows audit data can run outside the kernel as long as it cannot alter policy or the audit trail, which keeps the kernel small."},
{"id":"m38","d":2,"q":"A SaaS provider lets each tenant upload custom workflow scripts. Last month one tenant's script looped and exhausted memory on a shared worker node, delaying jobs for 200 other tenants. Tenant data is already in separate schemas with per-tenant encryption keys, and the API gateway enforces per-tenant rate limits. The new requirement says a defect in one tenant's workflow must not affect others. Which approach BEST meets it?","o":["Tighten per-tenant API rate limits at the gateway so a tenant cannot flood the platform","Run each tenant's workflows in its own sandbox with CPU, memory, and runtime quotas","Move each tenant's data into a dedicated database instance with its own credentials","Rotate per-tenant encryption keys more often and keep them in separate key vaults"],"a":[1],"e":"The failure was a runaway script consuming shared compute, so per-tenant execution isolation with resource quotas, the bulkhead pattern, addresses it directly. Rate limits control request volume, not what a single script does once running. Separate databases and keys protect data confidentiality but do not stop a compute exhaustion fault from spreading."},
{"id":"m39","d":2,"q":"A federal agency's system team has finished categorizing a new case management system as moderate. They are now choosing the SP 800-53B baseline, applying a privacy overlay, removing controls that do not apply to cloud-inherited services, and adding compensating controls where a legacy component cannot meet a requirement. The ISSO also started drafting a contingency plan. Which NIST RMF step are they mainly performing?","o":["Categorize","Select","Implement","Assess"],"a":[1],"e":"Choosing a baseline, applying overlays, tailoring, and identifying compensating controls are all tasks in the Select step. Categorize ended when impact levels were set. Implement is when the chosen controls are put in place and documented. Assess is when an assessor tests whether the controls work."},
{"id":"m40","d":2,"q":"A federal benefits system's assessment found three high-risk findings. The system owner has a POA&M with fixes due in 90 days and wants to go live next week because a statutory deadline applies. The agency CISO recommends going live with extra monitoring, and the assessor's report is complete. The CIO's office is reorganizing and two division heads are arguing over budget. Who has the authority to accept the residual risk and permit operation?","o":["The system owner, who funds the system and owns the POA&M","The authorizing official named for this system by the agency","The agency CISO, who leads the overall information security program","The security control assessor, who produced the assessment"],"a":[1],"e":"Under the RMF the authorizing official is the senior official who formally accepts residual risk and grants authorization to operate. The system owner prepares the package and POA&M. The CISO advises and runs the program but does not accept risk for the mission. The assessor provides an independent view of control effectiveness. The reorganization is noise."},
{"id":"m41","d":2,"q":"A federal grants system processes two information types. Using SP 800-60 provisional levels, grant applications are moderate confidentiality, low integrity, low availability. Payment scheduling data is low confidentiality, moderate integrity, high availability. The agency documented and approved an adjustment lowering payment availability to moderate because a tested manual process covers outages of up to five days. What is the resulting categorization and SP 800-53B baseline?","o":["Overall high, using the high baseline, because one provisional value was high","Moderate confidentiality and integrity, with high controls applied for availability","Overall moderate, using the moderate baseline after the approved adjustment","Overall low, because most of the individual impact values are rated as low"],"a":[2],"e":"After the documented adjustment, the high water mark across information types is moderate for each objective, so the system is moderate and uses the moderate baseline. Using the provisional high ignores the approved adjustment. FIPS 200 picks one baseline from the overall high water mark rather than mixing levels per objective. Counting how many values are low is not how categorization works."},
{"id":"m42","d":2,"q":"A company is building attribute-based authorization for its HR applications using an XACML-style architecture. Policies are written centrally, application gateways intercept requests, and a central engine evaluates the policies. A new rule says access to salary data requires completion of this year's privacy training, which is tracked in the learning management system. The gateways are already deployed. Which component should supply the training status at decision time?","o":["The policy administration point, where the training rule is authored","The policy decision point, by storing training records in its own cache","The policy information point, connected to the learning system's data","The policy enforcement point, by adding training status to each request"],"a":[2],"e":"A policy information point retrieves attribute values from authoritative sources when the PDP needs them, so it is the right place to connect the learning system. The PAP is where policies are written. Copying training records into the PDP creates a stale second source. Having each gateway gather attributes spreads logic across enforcement points and duplicates effort."},
{"id":"m43","d":2,"q":"A requirement says all administrative actions on 3,000 network switches must be attributable to an individual. Today engineers share a local admin account. The switches support TACACS+, and the company already runs Cisco ISE. The privileged access management vault is licensed for only 200 targets and is fully used by servers. An auditor visits next quarter. Which design BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Check the shared switch password out of the PAM vault per session, with session recording","Individual TACACS+ accounts with MFA and per-command accounting sent to a protected log","Restrict switch management to two jump hosts and record the jump host sessions centrally","Rotate the shared local password monthly and log who requested it from the help desk"],"a":[1],"e":"Individual TACACS+ accounts tie each command to a person, and ISE already provides the service, so it scales to 3,000 switches. A PAM checkout can provide attribution but the vault has no capacity. Jump hosts help with control and recording but attribution still depends on a shared device account. Tracking who asked for a shared password is weak evidence of who acted."},
{"id":"m44","d":2,"q":"A review finds that one online issuing CA signs internal TLS server certificates, code signing certificates for the release pipeline, and smart card logon certificates. The CA key is already in an HSM, certificates have one-year lifetimes, and OCSP responders are deployed. The PKI team has two engineers and a renewal cycle due in eight months. What is the BEST architectural change to address the main concern?","o":["Shorten certificate lifetimes to 90 days and fully automate renewal across all three uses","Separate issuing CAs by purpose under an offline root, with EKU and name constraints","Add OCSP stapling and a short CRL publishing interval to speed up revocation","Move the CA's HSM into a second data center to make the CA highly available"],"a":[1],"e":"The main concern is blast radius: one compromised CA undermines TLS, code signing, and user logon together. Separate purpose-specific issuing CAs under an offline root, limited by EKU and name constraints, contain a compromise. Shorter lifetimes and faster revocation help recovery but do not split trust. High availability addresses outages, not compromise."},
{"id":"m45","d":2,"q":"A payment company expanded into two new countries this year, and its board lowered its risk appetite for fraud loss. The security architecture for the payment platform was approved two years ago. The platform team already runs cloud security posture management with daily drift reports, and an external firm does an annual pen test. What practice BEST keeps the architecture aligned with the changing business requirements?","o":["Extend CSPM coverage to the new regions and add their local policies to the daily drift reports","Commission the annual penetration test to cover the new countries' payment flows","Hold an architecture review at each major release, focused on new components only","Reassess on business change and on a schedule, tracing controls to business attributes"],"a":[3],"e":"Alignment with business needs requires governance that reassesses the architecture when drivers like markets or risk appetite change, with traceability from business attributes to controls. CSPM checks configuration against existing policy, not whether the policy still fits the business. A pen test finds technical weaknesses. Reviews tied only to new components miss changes in requirements."},
{"id":"m46","d":2,"q":"A manufacturer runs software development and production on one large host that supports lattice-based mandatory labels in the kernel. Auditors want developers kept out of production data, production users kept away from development tools, and untested code kept from reaching production unless a certified process moves it. The team does not want to build application-level transaction procedures. The host's lease ends in three years. Which model fits BEST?","o":["Clark-Wilson, using certified transformation procedures to promote code to production","Biba strict integrity, with production assigned a higher integrity level than development","Lipner, which combines Bell-LaPadula and Biba levels and categories in one lattice","Brewer-Nash, placing development and production data in one conflict-of-interest class"],"a":[2],"e":"Lipner combines Bell-LaPadula for confidentiality separation and Biba for integrity, using the kernel's labels and categories for development, production, and system roles. Clark-Wilson fits commercial integrity well but relies on transformation procedures, which the team ruled out. Biba alone does not address keeping developers out of production data. Brewer-Nash handles conflicts of interest based on access history."},
{"id":"m47","d":2,"q":"A fintech built a custom authorization engine where administrators can define new commands that create subjects, create objects, and add or remove rights under conditions. The risk committee wants a formal proof that a contractor can never gain the 'approve payment' right. A consultant cites the Harrison-Ruzzo-Ullman result. Which response BEST reflects what HRU implies for this design?","o":["Model-check the current access matrix, since a finite matrix makes the question decidable","Restrict the commands, for example to mono-operational ones, so that safety becomes decidable","Route rights changes through a reference monitor, which makes safety decidable in general","Rely on quarterly access recertification, since HRU shows leaks surface only after the fact"],"a":[1],"e":"HRU showed that safety is undecidable for general access-matrix systems, but decidable for restricted forms such as mono-operational commands. The current matrix is finite, but commands can create new subjects and objects without bound, so checking today's matrix does not settle it. A reference monitor enforces policy but does not change decidability. Recertification is useful but is not a formal answer."},
{"id":"m48","d":2,"q":"An architect at a hospital using SABSA has finished the business attributes profile and control objectives. The next deliverable defines security services such as entity authentication, audit, and confidentiality protection, plus the policy architecture and security domains, all described independently of any product or vendor. Procurement is already asking which SIEM to buy. At which SABSA layer is the architect now working?","o":["Conceptual layer, the architect's view","Logical layer, the designer's view","Physical layer, the builder's view","Component layer, the tradesman's view"],"a":[1],"e":"Product-independent security services, the security policy architecture, and domain definitions belong to the Logical layer, the designer's view. The Conceptual layer is where the attributes profile and control objectives were produced. The Physical layer describes mechanisms and platforms. The Component layer names specific products, which is where the SIEM question would be answered."},
{"id":"m49","d":2,"q":"A retailer's architecture team built a security capability model covering identity governance, detection, data protection, and response, each rated on a five-level maturity scale. The board has asked for a three-year security investment plan tied to the company's risk appetite. The security operations lead also wants to use the model to retire overlapping tools. What is the MOST important architectural use of the model for the board's request?","o":["Map each deployed tool to a capability to find overlap and retire redundant licenses","Compare current against target maturity by risk to find gaps and order investment","Use the capability ratings as the control baseline for next year's internal audit","Benchmark the maturity scores against industry peers to justify the budget level"],"a":[1],"e":"For an investment plan tied to risk appetite, the key use is comparing current and target maturity, weighting the gaps by risk, and sequencing funding. Tool rationalization is a valid secondary use but answers a cost question, not the board's. A capability model is not a control baseline. Peer benchmarks can support a budget but do not tie spending to this company's risks."},
{"id":"m50","d":2,"q":"A bank is designing a real-time payment service where payments settle and become irrevocable within seconds. The threat model highlights an insider in payment operations initiating fraudulent transfers. Operations staff already use privileged access management for server logins, and their workstations have DLP agents. A UEBA tool is being piloted by the SOC. Which control set BEST addresses this threat?","o":["Extend privileged access management to operator sessions with full session recording","Use UEBA alerts on unusual payment patterns with a SOC runbook for rapid follow-up","Tighten DLP policies on operations workstations to block bulk export of account data","Require maker-checker approval above set limits, with fraud scoring done before release"],"a":[3],"e":"Because real-time payments are irrevocable, the control must stop the fraudulent payment before release. Maker-checker approval and pre-release scoring do that. Session recording on PAM covers server administration, not business transactions in the payment application. UEBA alerts arrive after settlement. DLP addresses data theft, not fraudulent payments."},
{"id":"m51","d":2,"q":"A vendor says its new access gateway delivers zero trust. Users authenticate with MFA at login, and the gateway then grants access to all internal applications for eight hours without further checks, even if the device falls out of compliance. Traffic between the gateway and applications is encrypted. The vendor's dashboard is popular with the help desk. Which change would MOST align the design with NIST SP 800-207?","o":["Evaluate each resource request per session using current identity and device posture","Require phishing-resistant FIDO2 authentication at the initial login to the gateway","Microsegment the data center VLANs so applications are reachable only via the gateway","Encrypt traffic on the internal network between application servers and databases"],"a":[0],"e":"SP 800-207 calls for access to each resource to be granted per session and decided dynamically using current signals such as device posture. One check at login followed by eight hours of broad access misses that. Stronger login MFA still checks once. Microsegmentation helps but is network-centric. Traffic is already encrypted to the applications, so more encryption does not fix the gap."},
{"id":"m52","d":2,"q":"A field service app connects directly to a backend database over an exposed port, using one shared database account, and the app decides which records each technician may see. The database engine version lacks row-level security and the vendor has ended support. Technicians connect from carrier networks with changing IP addresses. Leadership wants a fix that addresses the root cause. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Obfuscate the app and add certificate pinning so the authorization logic is harder to tamper with","Move the database to a non-standard port and allow connections only from known carrier ranges","Give each technician a personal database account and use views to limit what each one can query","Add an API tier that authenticates users and enforces authorization, and close the database port"],"a":[3],"e":"The root flaw is that enforcement sits on a client the attacker controls. A server-side API tier that authenticates and authorizes each request, with the database no longer exposed, fixes that. Obfuscation and pinning slow attackers but leave enforcement on the client. Port changes and carrier ranges are weak against changing IPs. Per-user accounts with views on an unsupported engine still expose the database directly."},
{"id":"m53","d":2,"q":"A hospital wants access to records to depend on the clinician's role, the patient's current ward, the time relative to the clinician's shift, and whether an active treatment relationship exists. Policies change often after compliance reviews, and the hospital does not want to redeploy applications when they do. The current system uses RBAC with about 1,800 roles. Which access control approach fits BEST?","o":["RBAC with additional roles defined for each ward and each shift pattern","Attribute-based control evaluated against subject, resource, and context","Relationship-based control built on a graph of clinicians and patients","Mandatory access control with sensitivity labels assigned per record"],"a":[1],"e":"ABAC evaluates policies over subject, resource, and environment attributes at request time, so role, ward, shift timing, and treatment status can all be combined and changed centrally. More roles would make the existing role explosion worse. Relationship-based control handles the treatment link but not time and location well. MAC labels classify data sensitivity and do not express these dynamic conditions."},
{"id":"m54","d":2,"q":"A team releases a claims platform weekly through CI/CD. Most releases are small UI changes, but every few months the team adds a new integration or data store. The pipeline already runs SAST, DAST, and dependency scanning, and an annual pen test is in the budget. The security lead has four hours a month for threat modeling. What should the architect recommend for threat modeling?","o":["Run a full threat model each quarter on a fixed schedule regardless of what each release contains","Model once at design and add a review of the model to the scope of each yearly pen test","Update the model when data flows or trust boundaries change, with a quick check per story","Rely on the pipeline's SAST and DAST results to flag new threats as each release ships"],"a":[2],"e":"A living model updated on architectural change, with a light check on each story to spot such changes, focuses the limited time where risk actually shifts. A fixed quarterly cycle can miss a mid-quarter integration and wastes time when little changed. A yearly pen test review finds design gaps late. Scanners find code flaws but do not identify design-level threats."},
{"id":"m55","d":2,"q":"A vendor is designing a secure appliance on a processor with four hardware privilege rings. Device drivers run in an intermediate ring, applications in the outermost ring, and the kernel in the innermost. Reviewers require that code in an outer ring can enter more privileged code only at fixed, validated entry points rather than jumping to arbitrary kernel addresses. The appliance also uses DMA-capable network cards. Which mechanism meets this requirement?","o":["Page table supervisor bits that mark kernel memory inaccessible to user code","Gate descriptors that define the allowed entry points into the inner rings","An IOMMU that confines device DMA to assigned regions of physical memory","Separate per-process address spaces enforced by the memory management unit"],"a":[1],"e":"Call gates, or similar gate descriptors, define fixed entry points through which outer rings can transfer control inward, which is exactly the requirement. Supervisor bits protect memory from access but do not define entry points. An IOMMU addresses the DMA risk, which is a separate concern. Per-process address spaces isolate processes from each other, not ring transitions."},
{"id":"m56","d":2,"q":"A payments API must meet a non-repudiation requirement for transfers above one million dollars, so that the bank can prove to a third party that a specific corporate client authorized each one. Today clients use API keys over TLS 1.3. The client team proposes moving to mutual TLS with client certificates, and the bank already logs each request with the key ID. Which design BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Switch to mutual TLS with client certificates so each session is tied to the client","Sign requests with an HMAC of the API secret and keep the signatures in the log","Have clients sign each transfer with a private key only they hold, plus timestamps","Expand request logs with key IDs, source IPs, and payload hashes in a WORM store"],"a":[2],"e":"Non-repudiation needs evidence a third party can verify that only the client could have produced, which is a digital signature over each transaction made with a key only the client holds, backed by trusted timestamps. Mutual TLS authenticates the channel but does not leave a signed record of each message. HMAC uses a shared secret the bank also has. Better logs still rest on a shared key."},
{"id":"m57","d":2,"q":"After a ransomware incident, investigators found the attacker phished a user, then captured domain admin credentials from the same workstation when a help desk technician logged on with a domain admin account to fix a printer. The company already uses LAPS for local admin passwords and has EDR on all endpoints. Which architectural change MOST directly prevents this path?","o":["Enable Credential Guard on workstations to protect cached secrets in memory","Tune EDR detections for credential dumping tools and alert the SOC faster","Adopt an admin tier model that blocks domain admin logons on workstations","Shorten LAPS password lifetimes and require approval for each password retrieval"],"a":[2],"e":"A tiered model with logon restrictions stops tier 0 credentials from being used on tier 2 systems, which removes the exposure that enabled this attack. Credential Guard protects some secrets in memory, but an attacker on the machine can still capture credentials typed into it. EDR tuning is detective. LAPS covers local admin accounts, not domain admin."},
{"id":"m58","d":2,"q":"A water utility is applying ISA/IEC 62443-3-2 to its treatment plant. The team has defined the system under consideration, completed an initial high-level risk assessment, and partitioned the plant into zones and conduits based on the Purdue levels. The board wants to start buying certified components, and the SOC wants IDS sensors on each conduit. What should the team do NEXT under the standard?","o":["Buy controllers certified to 62443-4-2 for the zones that hold safety equipment","Deploy IDS sensors on each conduit so traffic between zones can be monitored","Run a detailed risk assessment for each zone and assign target security levels","Re-map the assets to Purdue levels zero through five to confirm zone boundaries"],"a":[2],"e":"In 62443-3-2, after partitioning into zones and conduits, the next step is a detailed risk assessment per zone and conduit and assignment of target security levels, which then drive requirements. Buying certified components comes after you know the required levels. IDS sensors are a control choice that also follows. The Purdue mapping was already used to draw the zones."},
{"id":"m59","d":2,"q":"During pre-production validation, a red team found that a firewall rule allows traffic from a broad partner object group to a payments subnet. The approved design specified a narrow rule allowing only two partner hosts. The change ticket shows an engineer reused an existing object group to save time. The release is scheduled in ten days. What is the BEST response?","o":["Treat it as a design flaw and revise the approved design to account for partner object groups","Treat it as an implementation defect, fix the rule, and add design-to-config checks to CI","Add an IPS signature set for the payments subnet to compensate for the broader rule","Re-run the threat model for the partner flows first, before deciding how to change the rule"],"a":[1],"e":"The design was correct and the build did not match it, so this is a verification failure. Fix the rule and add checks that compare configuration to the approved design so it does not recur. Revising the design treats a correct spec as wrong. An IPS compensates for a defect that can simply be fixed. The threat model already led to the right rule."},
{"id":"m60","d":2,"q":"A hospital's clinical apps authenticate against a single on-premises identity provider. A recent four-hour outage halted medication administration. The CMO insists on keeping individual accountability for every chart entry, and the CISO refuses to weaken authentication strength. The hospital has two data centers and a cloud tenant. Which design BEST addresses the availability risk?","o":["Sealed break-glass accounts shared per ward, with use reviewed after each outage","Redundant IdP instances across failure domains with bounded, audited local caching","A cloud IdP with a 99.99 percent SLA as the only identity provider for the apps","Clinical session timeouts extended to 12 hours to ride through most short IdP outages"],"a":[1],"e":"Redundant identity providers in separate failure domains, plus bounded and audited local credential caching, keep authentication strong and individual during outages. Shared break-glass accounts lose individual accountability. A single cloud IdP depends on the WAN link. Long sessions weaken authentication and do not help users who need to log in during an outage."},
{"id":"m61","d":2,"q":"The business owner of a new loan origination system submitted a single security requirement: 'The system must be secure.' No business attributes, risk appetite, or threat model exist for the system yet. The development team suggests adopting OWASP ASVS Level 2 as the acceptance standard, and compliance wants a list of NIST SP 800-53 controls. Design starts in three weeks. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Adopt OWASP ASVS Level 2 as the acceptance test standard for the application","Map the system to an SP 800-53 moderate baseline and list the selected controls","Run a short workshop with the owner to capture attributes, assets, and threats","Write abuse cases for the loan workflow so testers have concrete scenarios"],"a":[2],"e":"A vague requirement has to be grounded in what the business values and fears before it can become specific, measurable requirements. A short elicitation workshop does that and then informs which standards or controls fit. ASVS and an 800-53 baseline are reasonable later choices but picking them first skips the business context. Abuse cases depend on knowing the assets and threats."},
{"id":"m62","d":2,"q":"In Phase D of a TOGAF ADM cycle, an architect specified an enterprise key management capability: HSM-backed keys, dual control for key export, and FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validation. During Phase E the team picked a specific cloud provider's managed HSM service to deliver it. A finance analyst asked why the two documents use different language. Which statement about these items is MOST accurate?","o":["The capability specification is an ABB, and the cloud HSM service is an SBB identified in Phase E","The capability specification is an SBB because it states FIPS levels, and the product is its ABB","Both are ABBs, since TOGAF treats products as SBBs only after deployment in Phase G","The capability specification is an ABB from Phase E, and the product is an SBB from Phase F"],"a":[0],"e":"Architecture building blocks describe required capability, even with specific requirements like FIPS levels, and are developed in Phases B to D. Solution building blocks are the products or components that realize them, identified in Phase E. Stating a FIPS level does not make a spec an SBB. Products are SBBs when chosen, not only after deployment. The phase pairing in the last option is wrong."},
{"id":"m63","d":2,"q":"A CISO must decide next year's control investments and wants quantitative evidence of how well current defenses prevent and detect a realistic set of attacker techniques relevant to the company's industry. The board wants the measurement repeated after changes to show progress. A goal-based red team engagement was completed last year and found one path to domain admin. Which approach BEST meets the CISO's needs?","o":["A new goal-based red team engagement focused on reaching the crown jewel systems","Breach and attack simulation mapped to ATT&CK, measuring prevention and detection","An ATT&CK coverage heat map compiled from each security vendor's product claims","A tabletop exercise walking leaders through a ransomware scenario and response"],"a":[1],"e":"Breach and attack simulation, often run with a purple team, measures prevention and detection across many mapped techniques and can be repeated to show progress. A goal-based red team finds a path but does not measure broad coverage. Vendor claims show possible coverage, not what is actually configured and working. A tabletop tests decisions, not control performance."},
{"id":"m64","d":2,"q":"A company's main ERP instance enforces that no user can hold both the vendor maintenance role and the payment approval role. A newly acquired subsidiary has only three finance staff, and each must hold both roles to cover absences. External auditors still require a preventive control so that no one approves payment to a vendor they created or changed. Monthly reviews of vendor changes already occur. Which mechanism BEST fits the subsidiary?","o":["Static separation of duties applied to the two roles, as in the main ERP instance","Dynamic separation of duties that blocks approval of payments to vendors the user changed","Mandatory vacation for each finance employee, with duties reassigned while they are away","Expanded monthly review of vendor master changes with a second reviewer from the parent"],"a":[1],"e":"Dynamic separation of duties lets users hold both roles but blocks the conflicting action on the same object at run time, which fits a three-person team and is preventive. Static SoD would stop the staff from covering for each other. Mandatory vacations and monthly reviews are detective controls, and the auditors asked for a preventive one."},
{"id":"m65","d":2,"q":"An architecture team writes a document titled 'Exposing internal APIs to partners.' It states the recurring problem, the context where it applies, the competing forces such as latency against inspection depth, a solution using a gateway with token exchange, and the resulting trade-offs. Several project teams have reused it. The document is stored in the same wiki as the cloud standards. Which type of artifact is this?","o":["A reference architecture for the partner integration domain","A security standard for partner API integrations","A security pattern for a recurring design problem","A solution architecture for one partner project"],"a":[2],"e":"Problem, context, forces, solution, and consequences is the classic pattern structure, and it solves one recurring problem. A reference architecture is a broader blueprint that combines many patterns for a domain. A standard states mandatory requirements, not trade-offs. A solution architecture is specific to one project, while this is reused across many."},
{"id":"m66","d":2,"q":"During a resilience review, an architect learns that the month-end close process depends on a file transfer interface whose security properties are undocumented. Its owner left the company last year, it uses an old SFTP server, and nobody knows which partners connect to it. Month-end close is in nine days. A modernization program would replace it in about a year. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Block the interface at the firewall until its purpose and users are fully understood","Run an authenticated vulnerability scan and penetration test against the SFTP server","Fast-track the interface into the modernization program and replace it this quarter","Assign an accountable owner and document its flows, partners, and its trust assumptions"],"a":[3],"e":"The core risk is an unowned, undocumented dependency, so the first step is to give it an owner and capture what it does, who connects, and what it trusts. Blocking it would break month-end close. Scanning is useful once you know the scope and owner. Fast-tracking replacement without understanding the interface risks missing hidden dependencies."},
{"id":"m67","d":2,"q":"An election authority is designing an online voting pilot. Each eligible voter must be able to cast exactly one ballot, ballots must be counted correctly, and nobody, including the authority, should be able to link a ballot back to a voter. Coercion resistance is a stated goal. The pilot also needs accessible interfaces for voters with disabilities. Which technique BEST reconciles eligibility checking with ballot anonymity?","o":["Store the voter ID with each ballot, encrypted under the authority's key","Issue blind-signed voting credentials that the authority cannot link","Give voters receipts showing their choices so they can verify counting","Attach a hash of the voter's ID to each ballot to detect duplicates"],"a":[1],"e":"Blind signatures let the authority confirm eligibility and sign a credential without seeing it, so the credential later proves eligibility without revealing who used it. Encrypting voter IDs under the authority's key lets the authority link ballots. Receipts showing choices enable coercion and vote buying. Hashes of voter IDs can be reversed by hashing the voter roll."},
{"id":"m68","d":2,"q":"A medical device company is choosing an open-source cryptographic library for a product sold to US hospitals and federal health agencies. The candidates are a widely starred library with no validation, a newer memory-safe library with no audits yet, a commercially supported fork with an SLA, and a mature library with a validated FIPS 140-3 module and published audits. Which choice gives the STRONGEST assurance for this product?","o":["The widely starred library, given its large user base and many contributors","The newer memory-safe library, since it avoids whole classes of memory bugs","The commercially supported fork, since the SLA covers fixes and response time","The mature library with a validated FIPS module, audits, and active maintainers"],"a":[3],"e":"Independent audits, active maintenance, a solid vulnerability response history, and a validated FIPS 140-3 module give the most evidence, and federal buyers need FIPS validation. Popularity is not assurance. Memory safety is a real benefit but no audit or validation exists yet. An SLA covers support response, not the quality of the cryptography."},
{"id":"m69","d":2,"q":"A bank is choosing a contractor to perform security engineering for a new core banking program. Procurement asks which evidence best shows the maturity of the contractor's security engineering processes across projects. One bidder offers a Common Criteria certificate for its own product, another offers ISO 27001 certification, a third offers a SOC 2 Type II report, and a fourth offers an SSE-CMM appraisal. Which evidence BEST answers procurement's question?","o":["A Common Criteria certificate for the bidder's own security product","An ISO/IEC 27001 certificate for the bidder's corporate ISMS","A SOC 2 Type II report on the bidder's hosted service controls","An ISO/IEC 21827 SSE-CMM appraisal of the bidder's processes"],"a":[3],"e":"SSE-CMM measures the capability maturity of an organization's security engineering processes, which is what procurement asked about. A CC certificate gives assurance about one product. ISO 27001 certifies an information security management system, not engineering practice. SOC 2 reports on controls at a service organization over a period."},
{"id":"m70","d":2,"q":"A law firm is designing a document sharing platform. Attorneys want to share their own matter files with colleagues at their discretion, without IT tickets. The firm's general counsel requires that documents labeled 'privileged' cannot be shared outside the firm, even by the attorney who owns them. The firm uses Microsoft 365 and has 300 attorneys in four offices. Which access control approach BEST fits?","o":["Role-based access by practice group, with membership managed by the IT team","Owner-controlled sharing limited by a mandatory label rule on external sharing","Owner-controlled sharing with training and quarterly reviews of external shares","Mandatory labels on all files, with IT approving each request to share a file"],"a":[1],"e":"Discretionary sharing meets the attorneys' need, and a mandatory, label-based rule that owners cannot override enforces the privileged restriction. Practice group roles remove owner discretion. Training and reviews detect problems after the fact. Full MAC with IT approval for each share is the ticket-driven process the attorneys want to avoid."},
{"id":"m71","d":2,"q":"A cloud-first company has about 300 product teams deploying through Terraform and Kubernetes. The security architecture team has eight people and a set of written principles such as 'no public storage buckets' and 'workloads use managed identities.' Design reviews currently take three weeks and teams are starting to bypass them. Which approach BEST scales consistent evaluation against the principles?","o":["Train a security champion in each team to review designs against the principles","Encode the principles as policy-as-code checks in pipelines, with review of exceptions","Require the central team to review each design before the first deployment to production","Publish the principles in the wiki and require each team to self-attest to compliance"],"a":[1],"e":"Policy as code checks every change automatically and consistently, leaving the small central team to handle exceptions and new patterns. Security champions help but manual review varies between people. Central review is the bottleneck that is already being bypassed. Self-attestation gives no real evidence."},
{"id":"m72","d":2,"q":"Using SABSA, a hospital's architect is now defining how security will be run day to day: incident handling procedures, user provisioning operations, monitoring and reporting, and the performance measures that check each business attribute target is being met. The product choices were completed last month, and the help desk is switching ticketing tools. Which SABSA layer is the architect working in?","o":["Physical layer, the builder's view of mechanisms","Component layer, the tradesman's view of products","Operational layer, the service manager's view","Logical layer, the designer's view of services"],"a":[2],"e":"The Operational layer covers security service management: running, monitoring, and measuring the architecture over time, including checking attribute targets. The Physical layer covers mechanisms. The Component layer covers specific products, which were finished last month. The Logical layer defines services and policy independent of products."},
{"id":"m73","d":2,"q":"Two product teams rated the same session fixation threat using DREAD. One team scored Discoverability 2 and Exploitability 3, while the other scored them 9 and 8, so the threat landed in different priority bands across the portfolio. Both teams followed the same template, and both used the threat model tool's latest version. Which DREAD limitation does this example MOST clearly show?","o":["It leaves out privacy harms such as linkability of users","Scores depend heavily on who rates them and vary widely","It looks only at exploitability and ignores business damage","It applies mainly to web applications and not to other systems"],"a":[1],"e":"The example shows the main problem that led Microsoft to drop DREAD: ratings are subjective and inconsistent between people. Privacy coverage is a gap of many security frameworks but is not what the example shows. DREAD does include damage potential. It is not limited to web applications."},
{"id":"m74","d":2,"q":"A defense agency runs a TOP SECRET system that hosts two classified programs. Analysts cleared at TOP SECRET should see only the program they are read into, and data owners must not be able to grant access to people outside a program on their own. The system's MAC kernel supports a lattice of levels and categories. The agency is also planning a new badge system. Which mechanism BEST enforces this need to know?","o":["Owner-managed access control lists on each program's folders","Separate classification levels defined for each program","Categories in the security labels, one for each program","Roles for each program assigned by the program security officer"],"a":[2],"e":"Categories, or compartments, within the label enforce need to know at the same level, and the kernel enforces them so owners cannot override. Owner-managed ACLs are discretionary. Inventing new levels for programs breaks the meaning of the hierarchy. Roles can group users but are not part of the mandatory label check."},
{"id":"m75","d":2,"q":"A public health agency gives analysts an ad hoc SQL interface to aggregated case data so they can study outbreaks. Analysts can run unlimited, overlapping queries with different filters. Records are already pseudonymized and access is limited to approved staff. A privacy review warns that comparing results of similar queries could reveal individual cases. Which control BEST addresses this risk?","o":["Suppress each result cell that covers fewer than ten individual cases","Add calibrated noise to query results under a differential privacy budget","Replace the pseudonymous identifiers with fresh random tokens each month","Narrow analyst roles so that each analyst can query only one region"],"a":[1],"e":"Differential privacy bounds what any set of queries can reveal about one person, which defends against differencing attacks from overlapping queries. Minimum cell sizes help but can be defeated by subtracting the results of two similar large queries. Re-tokenizing identifiers does not affect aggregate inference. Narrower roles reduce scope but analysts can still difference within their region."},
{"id":"p1","d":2,"q":"A bank ships its mobile app every two weeks. Each release waits on a two-week manual penetration test, so features slip by a full cycle. The banking regulator expects at least one independent test per year. The AppSec team is one engineer, the backend is Kotlin on Kubernetes, and the product owner has asked security to stop being the bottleneck. Most releases are UI tweaks, but roughly one in five touches login, payments, or a new partner integration. What should the architect propose?","o":["Threat model at design, gate builds on SAST and SCA, and send only releases touching auth, payments, or trust boundaries to manual testing, plus the yearly test","Replace the manual test with a DAST scan that blocks on high findings in the release pipeline, and contract the yearly independent test to satisfy the regulator","Keep the manual test but run it in parallel with the next sprint, shipping first and folding the findings into the following release cycle","Deploy RASP on the Kotlin backend so releases can ship without pre-release testing while runtime protection blocks exploitation of whatever slips through"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk-based routing keeps delivery moving: automation covers common code flaws, threat modeling catches design flaws, and the scarce manual effort goes to the one in five releases that change sensitive paths. DAST alone misses design and logic flaws. Shipping before testing means sensitive changes reach production untested. RASP is a compensating runtime control, not a substitute for testing."},
{"id":"p17","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing an internal web framework that 60 product teams will adopt over the next year. Past audits found teams forgetting to enable CSRF protection and output encoding. Some teams build legitimate edge cases, such as webhook receivers that can't use CSRF tokens. Senior engineers warn that if the framework is too rigid, teams will fork it. Which approach BEST improves security outcomes?","o":["Ship security features as well-documented helper functions in the framework and require teams to call them in code review","Turn protections on by default with narrow per-route opt-outs that are logged and flagged for a security review","Lock the protections on with no override, so the framework rejects routes that try to disable CSRF or encoding","Keep the framework neutral and fund annual secure coding training so teams know when to enable each protection"],"a":[1],"e":"Secure-by-default with visible, reviewable exceptions gives protection without forgetting and still handles real edge cases like webhooks. Opt-in helpers depend on the same memory that failed in audits. A hard lock pushes teams with real edge cases to fork or work around the framework. Training helps but doesn't change the default behavior that caused the findings."},
{"id":"p19","d":2,"q":"A media company has about 200 development teams and a central AppSec group of six. Two years ago it ran a security champions program that faded within months because champions had no time set aside and their managers kept pulling them back to feature work. The CISO has budget for either three more AppSec hires or tooling. Developers say security reviews are the slowest part of delivery. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Use the budget for three AppSec hires focused on design reviews for the highest-risk teams, with others self-serving","Relaunch champions with protected time agreed by engineering leadership and clear goals, backed by paved-road tooling","Require an external penetration test before each major release, funded from the budget, to shift review load off AppSec","Buy an enterprise SAST and SCA platform with pipeline gates so automated checks replace most manual security reviews"],"a":[1],"e":"The program failed for a known, fixable reason: no time allocation or leadership backing. Fixing that, plus tooling that makes the secure path easy, is the only option that scales to 200 teams. Three hires still leave a 6-to-200 ratio. External tests per release add delay and cost. Tooling helps but can't replace design-level judgment inside teams."},
{"id":"p26","d":2,"q":"A company piloted a SAST tool on its flagship application and got 4,000 findings. Developers have stopped opening the reports, and two team leads called the tool 'noise.' The vendor contract runs three years. The application has about a million lines of code, and leadership wants a visible win within a quarter. What should the architect do NEXT?","o":["Gate new code on a small set of high-confidence rules and baseline the legacy findings for later triage","Configure the pipeline to fail builds on all high and critical findings so developers have to address them","Hire contractors to triage the full 4,000-finding backlog first, then hand verified results to developers","Run a bake-off with two other SAST vendors to find a tool with a lower false-positive rate on this codebase"],"a":[0],"e":"Starting with high-confidence rules on new code gives developers accurate, actionable results and stops new debt, while the backlog is triaged separately. Failing builds on the full set would halt delivery over noise. Triaging the backlog first delays any visible win and doesn't fix the experience going forward. Switching vendors in a three-year contract is costly and likely to recreate the same issue without tuning."},
{"id":"p29","d":2,"q":"A company acquired a smaller firm whose claims application processes sensitive customer data and must stay in production. There are no automated tests, no architecture documents, and the original developers have left. One contractor who knows the system is under contract for 30 more days. The acquirer's WAF and SIEM can be extended to it next quarter. The board wants a risk picture at next month's meeting. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Run full SAST and DAST scans against the codebase and staging environment to build a vulnerability baseline","Put the application behind the corporate WAF and SIEM ahead of schedule to get compensating controls in place","Work with the contractor to document architecture, data flows, and interfaces, then threat model the system","Begin scoping a rewrite on the acquirer's standard stack, since the code has no tests and unclear ownership"],"a":[2],"e":"The scarcest resource is the contractor's knowledge, which disappears in 30 days. Capturing architecture and data flows now enables a threat model, a board-ready risk picture, and better-targeted scans and controls later. Scans can run anytime and are hard to interpret without context. Accelerating WAF and SIEM is useful but can wait the few weeks. A rewrite decision is premature without understanding the system."},
{"id":"p39","d":2,"q":"A pen test of a new B2B portal found that users from one customer could see another customer's invoices because the design never defined how customer boundaries would be enforced. Fixing it now requires changing the data model and three services, two weeks before launch. The portal team used SAST in CI, DAST in staging, and peer code review. Leadership asks which activity, added to the process, would have caught this most cheaply.","o":["Threat modeling at design review, focused on trust boundaries and customer data separation","SAST rules in CI that flag database queries missing a customer ID filter in the WHERE clause","Authenticated DAST in staging with test accounts from two customers, run before each release","A secure code review checklist item asking reviewers to confirm customer filtering in data access code"],"a":[0],"e":"This is a design flaw: the isolation model was never defined. Threat modeling at design is where it would surface, before any code exists, so it's the cheapest point to fix. SAST rules can't tell what a correct filter is when the design doesn't define one. Two-customer DAST can detect it, but only after the code is built. A review checklist catches individual lapses, not a missing design decision."},
{"id":"p41","d":2,"q":"An architect is choosing runtime testing for a QA environment. The platform has 25 Java services and 15 Go services. QA runs automated functional tests that cover about 70% of features. The candidate IAST product supports Java but not Go. Leadership wants fewer false positives than the current SAST tool and results within the existing test cycle. Which plan fits BEST?","o":["Deploy IAST agents on all 40 services so the functional tests drive coverage across the whole platform","Run DAST against all services in QA and drop IAST, so one tool covers both languages the same way","Deploy IAST on the Java services driven by the functional tests, and run DAST against the Go services","Expand SAST to cover the Go services and use its results in place of runtime testing in the QA cycle"],"a":[2],"e":"IAST gives accurate, code-level findings with few false positives where it's supported, and the existing functional tests drive it inside the cycle. DAST fills the gap for Go. IAST can't instrument Go services with this product. DAST alone gives up IAST's precision on the Java majority. More SAST doesn't meet the goal of fewer false positives or runtime coverage."},
{"id":"p53","d":2,"q":"An agile team building a patient scheduling app works in two-week sprints. The company's application security standard is a 60-page document, and developers say they don't read it. Security issues keep turning up late in the cycle. The team has a security champion who attends refinement. The product owner is worried about velocity. Which approach BEST builds security into the team's work?","o":["Attach the full standard to each epic and have the champion confirm compliance at review","Run a quick threat model on risky stories in refinement and turn the results into acceptance criteria","Schedule a dedicated security hardening sprint each quarter to work down issues found late in the cycle","Have the central security team keep a separate security backlog that the team pulls from when it has capacity"],"a":[1],"e":"Turning a short threat model of each risky story into concrete acceptance criteria makes security part of normal 'done' and keeps it small enough to fit the sprint. Attaching the full standard repeats what already fails. Hardening sprints formalize finding issues late. A separate backlog makes security optional work that loses to features."},
{"id":"o5","d":2,"q":"An order management system has a BIA-approved RTO of 4 hours and RPO of 1 hour. The database ships transaction logs to a DR region every 15 minutes. Nightly full backups go to immutable storage. In DR, the application tier is rebuilt from infrastructure-as-code, which took 5 hours in the last test because of image builds and manual DNS changes. The DR region uses a different instance family than production. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["The design meets both targets, because immutable nightly backups give a clean recovery point inside the window","The RPO is met through log shipping, but the RTO is missed because the application tier takes 5 hours to rebuild","The RPO is missed because nightly backups set the recovery point, but the RTO is met by the replicated database","The RTO is met for data since the database is already in the DR region, and the app tier is outside the BIA target"],"a":[1],"e":"Log shipping every 15 minutes keeps data loss under the 1-hour RPO, but the service is not back until the app tier is up, and that took 5 hours, over the 4-hour RTO. Immutable backups matter for ransomware but do not set the RPO here. The nightly backup is not the primary recovery point when logs are shipped. RTO applies to the service, not just the database."},
{"id":"o8","d":2,"q":"A regional bank ran a DR tabletop last year and a checklist review this spring. It has never actually brought up systems at the DR site. The regulator now expects evidence that core banking can run from DR, but the business will not accept any planned interruption to production during the review period. The DR site was refreshed with new storage six months ago. Which test should the bank run NEXT?","o":["A full interruption test during a low-volume weekend, cutting production over to the DR site","Another tabletop with technical injects that walk through the new storage failover steps","A parallel test that brings DR systems up and processes real transactions without cutting over","A component test of the new storage replication alone, with results documented for the regulator"],"a":[2],"e":"A parallel test proves DR systems can run real workloads without touching production, which fits both the regulator and the no-interruption constraint. A full interruption test is the most realistic but the business ruled it out. Another tabletop does not show systems actually work. A storage component test is useful but does not show core banking runs from DR."},
{"id":"o10","d":2,"q":"A SOC reports 80% MITRE ATT&CK coverage based on how its detection rules are tagged. In a recent red team exercise that mimicked a ransomware group active in the sector, the SOC caught only two of seven attack stages. Leadership asks why the coverage number did not reflect reality. The SOC has a breach and attack simulation license that is mostly unused. What should the architect do NEXT?","o":["Buy a commercial detection content pack tagged to the techniques that currently show no coverage","Re-map existing rules at the sub-technique level so the coverage report reflects real precision","Run BAS tests across the whole ATT&CK matrix and use the pass rate as the new coverage metric","Run purple-team emulation of that group's techniques and measure which of the SOC's detections actually fire"],"a":[3],"e":"Tag-based coverage measures intent, not effectiveness. Emulating the techniques of the most relevant threat and checking what actually fires shows real gaps and lets the team fix them. More content adds tags without proof. Sub-technique re-mapping refines the same paper metric. Running BAS across the full matrix is broad but not prioritized by threat, and pass rate on generic tests can again overstate readiness."},
{"id":"o31","d":2,"q":"A company's backup dashboard showed green for every job for two years. During a ransomware recovery, restores of three key systems failed because the backup catalog was corrupted, and rebuilding it took two days. Auditors now want evidence that recovery works. Backups are written to a deduplicating appliance from the same vendor as the backup software. The finance team is asking for a cheaper storage tier. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["Copy backups to a second appliance from a different vendor so a single product flaw cannot hit both copies","Replicate the backup catalog to a separate system and alert on catalog consistency check failures","Schedule regular full restores of critical systems, verify the app works, and track results against RTO","Alert on job warnings as well as failures so that partial or skipped backups get reviewed by staff"],"a":[2],"e":"Only real restores, including bringing the application up, prove backups can meet recovery targets and give auditors evidence. A second vendor copy improves resilience but still is not tested. Protecting the catalog fixes this one failure cause but not others. Better job alerting still only measures backup completion, not recoverability."},
{"id":"o38","d":2,"q":"A company's IR plan has never been tested. The regulator has asked for evidence of testing within the year. The CEO wants an unannounced red team against production next month to see how the team really performs. Several playbooks were written by a consultant who has left, and two key responders joined last month. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Run the unannounced red team in production next month, as the CEO asked, for a realistic result","Do a tabletop with responders and executives first, then technical drills, then live exercises later","Have each team lead review the IR plan document and sign off that their section is accurate","Run a full simulated attack in production with all teams pre-briefed on the date and the scope"],"a":[1],"e":"An untested plan with new staff should start with a low-risk tabletop that exposes gaps in roles and decisions, then progress to technical and live tests. An unannounced red team now would mostly confirm the plan is untested, at higher risk. A document review alone does not test anything. A full production simulation is a later stage once basics are proven."},
{"id":"o41","d":2,"q":"A company runs an annual penetration test and a red team every two years. Firewall policies, EDR settings, and SIEM rules change weekly, and twice last year a change quietly broke detection for months. Leadership wants ongoing assurance that controls still detect and block common techniques after changes. The team uses a GRC tool for control attestations. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Move the penetration test to a quarterly schedule and include detection checks","Scan all systems weekly with authenticated vulnerability scans and track drift","Use breach and attack simulation on a schedule and check that alerts actually fire","Run an annual red team and purple team pair that produces a detection gap report each time"],"a":[2],"e":"BAS runs safe, repeatable attack simulations continuously or after changes and confirms detections and blocks still work, which is exactly the regression problem described. Quarterly pentests and annual red teams are valuable but too infrequent. Vulnerability scans find weaknesses, not detection failures."},
{"id":"o57","d":2,"q":"A company moved most workloads to SaaS and cloud over three years. Its SIEM still has the same 600 rules written for the on-premises environment, and leadership believes coverage is fine because alert volume is stable. The SIEM vendor offers a cloud content pack. The SOC also recently replaced its ticketing tool. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Buy the vendor's cloud content pack and enable its rules across all tenants","Migrate to a cloud-native SIEM with built-in connectors for SaaS platforms","Map current assets and threats to log sources and rules to find coverage gaps","Deploy a CSPM tool to find misconfigurations across the cloud accounts and SaaS"],"a":[2],"e":"Stable alert volume can hide the fact that old rules watch sources that barely matter now. Mapping today's assets and threats to what is collected and detected shows the real gaps first. A content pack or new SIEM may follow but is premature. CSPM finds misconfigurations, not detection gaps."},
{"id":"o59","d":2,"q":"A CISO wants a SOC maturity assessment to justify budget and to benchmark progress year over year. The company reports enterprise risk using the NIST Cybersecurity Framework. The SOC has 12 analysts, an MDR partner, and its own detection engineering. The board also asked about ISO 27001 certification. Which approach is MOST appropriate for the SOC assessment?","o":["Use NIST CSF implementation tiers alone, since they are already used at the enterprise level","Pursue ISO 27001 certification and use the audit results as the SOC maturity measure","Use SOC-CMM for a detailed SOC assessment, mapping results to the CSF for reporting","Measure ATT&CK detection coverage each year and report the percentage as maturity"],"a":[2],"e":"SOC-CMM is built to assess SOC people, process, technology, and services in detail, and mapping its results to CSF keeps board reporting consistent. CSF tiers are too high-level for SOC work. ISO 27001 certifies an ISMS, not SOC maturity. ATT&CK coverage is one technical measure, not overall maturity."},
{"id":"o75","d":2,"q":"The patch tool reports 99% compliance across 4,000 servers. An attacker still exploited a server marked patched, because a reboot was pending and the vulnerable code was still loaded. Audit wants a more reliable measure. The patch tool vendor offers a new dashboard module. Servers run Windows and Linux, and about 300 are in a newly acquired business unit. Which approach is MOST reliable?","o":["Use the patch tool's own compliance report, adding a pending-reboot column","Require server owners to attest monthly that patches are installed and active","Verify independently with authenticated scans or version checks against inventory","Monitor patch approval status in WSUS and in the Linux package repositories each week"],"a":[2],"e":"Independent checks of what is actually running, compared against the full inventory, catch pending reboots, failures, and missing assets. The patch tool reports its own view and may miss unmanaged servers. Attestation is manual. Approval status shows intent, not installed state."},
{"id":"o80","d":2,"q":"A company is acquiring a smaller firm and plans to connect the networks in 60 days. The target has an MSP-run firewall and EDR but no SOC, and reports no known incidents. The deal team wants assurance that the target is not already compromised. The target recently passed a PCI assessment, and its CEO says an outside review would be redundant. Which activity is MOST appropriate before connecting?","o":["Run authenticated vulnerability scans across the target's networks and fix critical gaps","Commission a penetration test of the target's perimeter and internal networks","Do a compromise assessment using threat hunting and forensic sweeps of the target","Review the MSP's EDR alert history and firewall logs for the past twelve months"],"a":[2],"e":"A compromise assessment looks for evidence of current or past intrusion, which is the question here. Vulnerability scans and pentests find weaknesses, not existing attackers. Reviewing MSP alerts only shows what the tools already caught. A PCI pass says little about compromise."}
);
